title: The Rentenmark
subtitle: The hyperinflation ends, and the workers pay for it.
new-page: true
tags: kpd_event
view-if: year = 1923 and month = 11 and y23_rentenmark_done = 0 and y23_chemnitz_done = 1
on-arrival: y23_rentenmark_done = 1; unemployed += 3; pro_republic -= 3

= The end of the inflation

In mid-November the new Rentenmark is introduced, secured on mortgages on land and industry. The old mark stops at 4.2 trillion to the dollar, and a trillion old marks are worth one Rentenmark. The printing presses stop and the prices stop rising. The government's solution is neither gentle nor fair: public servants are dismissed by the tens of thousands, the unemployment payments are cut, and in December the labour minister issues a decree that keeps the eight-hour day on paper and allows exceptions for longer hours almost everywhere.

The inflation wiped out the savings of the middle classes and the debts of the industrialists, and the stabilisation will be paid for by the workers. The money Stinnes and Krupp owed is gone, and the unemployed are told to be grateful.

